## Quick checklist for converting a CUDA kernel to RAJA

| Step | What to do |
|------|------------|
| 1 | Identify the iteration space (1‑D, 2‑D, 3‑D). |
| 2 | Replace `blockIdx`, `threadIdx`, `gridDim`, `blockDim` with RAJA policies (`cuda_exec`, `loop_exec`, etc.). |
| 3 | Use `RAJA::RangeSegment` (or other segment types) to describe the bounds. |
| 4 | Write the kernel body as a lambda marked `RAJA_DEVICE`. |
| 5 | Allocate device memory with `RAJA::cuda_malloc` and copy with `RAJA::cuda_memcpy`. |
| 6 | Synchronize with `RAJA::cuda_device_synchronize()` if needed. |
| 7 | Compile with a CUDA compiler and link against RAJA (`-lRAJA -lcudart`). |

