## Summary of Conversion Techniques

| CUDA Concept | RAJA Abstraction | How it Changes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `__global__ void kernel(...)` | Standard C++ function (`void kernel(...)`) | The kernel becomes a regular function body. |
| `<<<grid, block>>>` launch | `raja::parallel_for(start, end, lambda)` | The explicit launch configuration is replaced by a high-level loop construct. |
| `threadIdx`, `blockIdx` | Loop variable (`int i` in `parallel_for`) | RAJA automatically maps the loop iteration index to a unique work item ID. |
| `cudaMalloc`/`cudaFree` | `raja::device_vector<T>` | RAJA manages the device memory lifecycle for you, abstracting the calls. |
| `cudaMemcpy` | `device_vector::copy_from()` / `copy_to()` | RAJA provides overloaded methods on its memory types to handle data transfer. |

By adopting RAJA, your code becomes significantly more portable. If you later decide to switch from the CUDA backend to the HIP backend (for AMD GPUs) or even to a CPU OpenMP backend, you often only need to change a single line of code (the backend selection) without rewriting the core logic of your parallel loops.

